<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silvio Pellico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silvio Pellico was an Italian writer and patriot from Saluzzo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. Arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1820 for his links with the Carbonari, he spent ten years in Austrian prisons, mostly in the Spielberg fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>. His memoir of imprisonment, Le mie prigioni (1832), was read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and damaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s reputation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silvio-pellico.jpg" alt="Silvio Pellico" width="222" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silvio_Pellico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Silvio Pellico was born at Saluzzo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> on 24 June 1789 and spent his early life at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinerolo">Pinerolo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, taught by a priest named Manavella. At ten he wrote a tragedy inspired by a translation of the Ossianic poems. After his twin sister Rosina married in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> he lived there for four years, studying French literature. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1809 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, where he joined the circle around Ugo Foscolo and Vincenzo Monti, and in 1810 became professor of French at the Collegio degli Orfani Militari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His tragedy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesca-da-rimini">Francesca da Rimini</a>, staged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1815 and published three years later, was among the first and most successful inspired by Dante's episode in the fifth canto of the Inferno. Stendhal reviewed it enthusiastically and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> translated it into English. As a tutor he served the sons of Count Briche and of Count Porro Lambertenghi, and tried to weaken Austrian rule by indirect educational means. He was secretary and chief editor of the literary journal Il Conciliatore, which the Austrian censors allowed to run for only a year.</p><p>In October 1820 he was arrested on a charge of Carbonarism and held in the Santa Margherita prison, then moved to the Piombi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in February 1821. A sentence of death passed in February 1822 was commuted to fifteen years in harsh conditions, and in April he was placed in the Spielberg at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brünn</a>, today's Brno. There he composed the tragedy Leoniero da Dertona, which he had to keep in memory. He was released in 1830.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His account of his imprisonment, My Prisons, appeared in 1832 and gave him European fame. It was translated into virtually every European language in his lifetime and contributed to the movement for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> against Austrian occupation. It won him the friendship of the Marchesa Juliette Colbert de Barolo, a reformer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> prisons, who gave him a yearly pension in 1834. After his parents' deaths in 1838 he lived in her house, assisted her charities and wrote chiefly on religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>, best known of them the essay Dei doveri degli uomini. He died in Turin on 31 January 1854 and was buried in the Camposanto.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Silvio Pellico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lombardy-Venetia had an efficient administration, good schools and a prosperous economy, but censorship and police repression made Austria unpopular; the patriot <b>Silvio Pellico</b> described his imprisonment in the Spielberg fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lombardy-venetia">Kingdom of Lombardy-Venetia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saluzzo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.65,7.49&amp;year=1831" aria-label="Saluzzo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4729 585 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Silvio Pellico"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5179 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5179 855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.65,7.49&amp;year=1831">What was here in 1831? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
